---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';
import { getPracticeURL, withPracticeURL } from '../lib/practice';
import checkSample from '../samples/check-broken-short.txt?raw';
import mcpConfig from '../samples/mcp-config.json.txt?raw';
import walkSample from '../samples/sr-walk-broken.txt?raw';
import testSample from '../samples/vitest-home.ts.txt?raw';

const practiceURL = getPracticeURL(Astro);
const toc = [
   { id: 'install', label: 'Install' },
   { id: 'check', label: 'Scan a page' },
   { id: 'listen', label: 'Read it with a screen reader' },
   { id: 'test', label: 'Keep it fixed with a test' },
   { id: 'agent', label: 'Give the tools to an AI agent' },
   { id: 'results', label: 'What the results mean' },
   { id: 'next', label: 'Where to go next' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Test what a screen reader says"
   summary="<p>a11ied scans a web page for accessibility problems and shows you what a screen reader announces on it. Use it from the terminal, in your test suite, or through an AI coding agent.</p>"
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
   variant="home"
>
   <section>
      <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
      <pre><code>npm install --global a11ied</code></pre>
      <p>
         The command is <code>a1</code>. The examples below run against a
         <a href="/demo/team">practice page</a> with ten accessibility problems in it, so you
         can copy and run them unchanged.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="check">Scan a page</h2>
      <p>
         <code>a1 check</code> runs <a href="https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core">axe-core</a>,
         the open-source rule engine behind most accessibility scanners, in a real browser.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 check {practiceURL}</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{withPracticeURL(checkSample, practiceURL)}</code></pre>
      <p>
         Each problem names the <abbr title="Web Content Accessibility Guidelines">WCAG</abbr>
         criterion it fails, the element, and a command that explains the fix. Below the
         problems is the part most scanners leave out: the criteria this page triggers that
         no scanner can decide.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="listen">Read it with a screen reader</h2>
      <p>
         <code>a1 sr</code> drives a screen reader and prints what it announces.
         <code>sr walk</code> reads a whole page.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr walk {practiceURL} --sr virtual</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{withPracticeURL(walkSample, practiceURL)}</code></pre>
      <p>
         Line 46 is a button that says only "button". Line 15 is a search field with no label.
         A scanner reported the first and passed the second.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>sr walk</code> leaves the screen reader running for more commands. Stop it with
         <code>a1 sr stop</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>--sr virtual</code> is a simulated screen reader that needs no setup and runs
         anywhere. <code>--sr voiceover</code> on macOS and <code>--sr nvda</code> on Windows
         drive the real ones with the same commands. See
         <a href="/screen-readers">screen readers</a>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="test">Keep it fixed with a test</h2>
      <p>
         The same screen reader runs inside a test. This one opens a dialog and checks that
         the dialog announces its name and that focus moves into it.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="team.test.ts"><code>{withPracticeURL(testSample, practiceURL)}</code></pre>
      <p>
         See <a href="/guides/vitest">test with Vitest</a>, or
         <a href="/guides/testing">write screen reader tests</a> for Jest, Mocha, and
         Playwright Test.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="agent">Give the tools to an AI agent</h2>
      <p>
         A coding agent can write a dialog but cannot hear it. a11ied includes an
         <abbr title="Model Context Protocol">MCP</abbr> server that gives the agent every
         command above as a tool, so it can check its own work.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="CONFIG"><code>{mcpConfig.trimEnd()}</code></pre>
      <p>
         See <a href="/guides/agents">use with an AI agent</a>. To have agents run a full
         WCAG audit and write the report, see <a href="/guides/audit">run an audit</a>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="results">What the results mean</h2>
      <p>a11ied keeps three kinds of evidence apart, and so should your reports.</p>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt>A scan</dt>
         <dd>Finds failures. A clean scan does not mean a criterion passes, because no criterion can be decided by a scanner alone.</dd>
         <dt>A transcript</dt>
         <dd>Shows what a screen reader announced. A transcript from the simulated screen reader is not evidence of what VoiceOver or NVDA does.</dd>
         <dt>A judgment</dt>
         <dd>Decides the rest: whether alt text describes the image, whether focus order makes sense. A person or an agent makes it, and <code>a1 audit</code> records it.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>See <a href="/test-methods">test methods</a> for the numbers behind this.</p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="next">Where to go next</h2>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><a href="/quickstart">Quickstart</a></dt>
         <dd>Install, scan a page, and read it with a screen reader. Five minutes.</dd>
         <dt><a href="/guides/demo">Practice on a broken page</a></dt>
         <dd>Find all ten problems on the practice page. A scan reports three of them.</dd>
         <dt><a href="/guides/screen-reader">Test with a screen reader</a></dt>
         <dd>Every <code>a1 sr</code> command, with VoiceOver, NVDA, or the simulated screen reader.</dd>
         <dt><a href="/reference/cli">CLI reference</a></dt>
         <dd>Every command and option.</dd>
      </dl>
   </section>
</DocShell>
